<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro Cieza de León</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1520–<a href="/years/1554">1554</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro Cieza de León was a Spanish soldier and chronicler who spent some seventeen years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> from 1535. His Chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, the first part of which was published in 1553, describes the geography, peoples and history of the Andes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, drawing on interviews with Inca nobles. It is one of the most important early sources for Inca history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-cieza-de-leon.jpg" alt="Pedro Cieza de León" width="192" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_Cieza_de_Le%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cieza de León was born in Llerena, the son of Lope de León, a shopkeeper of the town, and Leonor de Cazalla, a native of Llerena. Little is known of his early life, and since he left home at 13 he probably had no more than a rudimentary education. Hearing in his teens of the discovery of the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> to sail for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and see for himself the riches of precious metal brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>. Alonso López and Luis de Torres attested that he was not barred from the colonies as a Jew or Jewish convert, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>'s secretary, Pedro López de Cazalla, was his first cousin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> he took part in many expeditions and helped found towns, among them San Ana de los Caballeros with Jorge Robledo in 1539, Cartago in 1540 and Antioquía in 1541, all in Popayán Province. In 1547 he joined the missions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-la-gasca">Pedro de la Gasca</a> in the royalist campaign against the rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a>. Reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, the City of Kings, in 1548, he began his work as a chronicler and spent two years travelling across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> gathering material, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> in August 1550. He recorded the sharply reduced population of almost every region and the violence of the Spaniards, and mourned the extortion and ill-treatment inflicted on the Indians. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in 1551 and married Isabel López de Abreu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His Crónicas del Perú was planned in four parts. The first, a survey of geography, customs and the Spanish foundations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, was reprinted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1554. The second, On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> of Yupanqui, appeared in 1871, translated by Clements Markham, and in Spanish at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1877; Marcos Jiménez de la Espada judged that much of it had been fraudulently appropriated by a later chronicler.</p><p>The third told the discovery and conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and a significant part of it was used by Antonio de Herrera y Tordesillas. The fourth, on the civil wars among the conquerors, was the longest. Raúl Porras called him the premier chronicler of the Indies, and he was the first European to describe some Peruvian animals and plants. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> on 2 July 1554, the year after the first part appeared.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pedro Cieza de León” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Accounts by <b>Pedro Cieza de León</b>, Juan de Betanzos, Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala and Garcilaso de la Vega, whose Comentarios Reales de los Incas appeared in 1609, made the Incas known in Europe, and Inti Raymi is still celebrated at Cusco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish chroniclers, among them <b>Pedro Cieza de León</b>, described its ruins in the sixteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tiwanaku">Tiwanaku · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Incas had no writing, only knotted-cord accounts (khipu), so the evidence comes from archaeology and from chroniclers writing after the conquest: <b>Pedro Cieza de León</b>, Juan de Betanzos, Garcilaso de la Vega and Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the chronicler <b>Pedro Cieza de León</b>, Guarco resisted the Incas for several years in the time of Túpac Inca Yupanqui.</blockquote><a href="/state/guarco">Guarco</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Llerena, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.24,-6.02&amp;year=1542" aria-label="Llerena on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4400 790 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pedro Cieza de León"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4850 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4850 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.24,-6.02&amp;year=1542">What was here in 1542? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
